{s:"microciv",t:"Micro Civ, a level",tag:"Level design, first-person",st:"Coursework, world from my own game",
sum:"A first-person level built in 1.5 weeks for an Environmental Level Design class during my Erasmus exchange, taken from the world of my own insect-civilization game. The ants, the trebuchet and the hollow log are my own models.",
f:{Role:"Level designer and builder",Time:"1.5 weeks",Rule:"Only a first-person controller and an audio trigger",Assets:"Free downloads, plus my own ants, trebuchet and hollow log",Source:"My own game, early development"},
b:"The task was to build an environment with a start area, an exploring area, a challenge area and an end area. Only objects and sound could lead the player to the finish, with no obvious guidance. The only tools allowed were the first-person controller and an audio trigger that makes objects appear or disappear inside a trigger zone. I made the ants, the trebuchet and the hollow log myself and used free assets for the rest.",
d:["Built the whole level from the world of my own insect-civilization game","Made the ants, the trebuchet and the hollow log myself, and dressed the rest of the world with free downloaded assets","Led the player with objects, glowing mushrooms and sound alone, along a main route with side branches","Made a challenge area with a maze and parkour: river stones and a tree climb","Ended it at a beehive in a tree, with a view over the whole location","Filled it with monologues, dialogues and small jokes that players can find by accident, especially in the maze"],
n:"Credits: much of the environment uses free downloaded assets. The ants, the trebuchet and the hollow log are my own work.",
video:"tqjq8EQxKPY",vh:"Full walkthrough",
ex:[["How the level guides the player", "Glowing mushrooms lead players mainly to interesting spots where something can be discovered, and overall toward the end of the maze. They guide without dictating the way. Inside the grass maze, the destination, the beehive, rises above the plants, so the player always has a rough sense of direction. Many small finds, dialogues and jokes along the way keep the player from getting bored."], ["Sound triggers", "The audio trigger was one of the two scripts we were allowed to use, next to the first-person controller. I attach a sound source to an object that has a trigger collider. When the player enters the zone, the sound plays, and with one checkbox the object also disappears or appears. In my level, the player picks up a letter, which disappears, and delivers it at the beehive at the end."], ["Testing and changes", "Testers, mostly classmates, gave me recommendations. In the first round I made the maze simpler and moved some objects depending on where players tended to go. A few more user-experience changes are still on my list."]],
hero:"mo",hc:"The level seen from above: river, forest clearing and the winding path.",
gh:"Around the level",
g:[["mm","Top-down map: the main route from start to end in red, side branches in yellow."],["ma","The start: an ant guard, one of my own models, sends you off with a letter for the Bee Palace."],["mb","The exploring area: a trebuchet I made, with its ant crew."],["mc","Dialogues hide small jokes, like ants waiting for their maggot soup."],["md","A shrine that hints without leading."],["mz","In the maze, glowing mushrooms mark the way."],["mf","Lines of dialogue reward those who explore."],["mg","The challenge area: river parkour over stones and lily pads."],["mh","The tree climb up to the beehive."],["mi","The end: the bee at the hive greets the letter-bearer."]],
r:"A complete, playable level with a video walkthrough. It could become a location in the finished game.",
rf:"I am happy with how this project turned out. In 1.5 weeks I built a full, detailed environment with a clear sense of purpose. Much could still be improved, but given the strict, short deadline I think I did well. Feedback from testers, mostly classmates, was largely positive, and playtesting also showed me a few things I would change to make the experience smoother for the player."},
{s:"zlatna",t:"Zlatna, rebrand and bottle design",tag:"Branding, product design",st:"Completed, real client",
sum:"A full rebranding and bottle redesign for Zlatna, a Croatian olive oil sold in Belgium, made for the International Business Project at KdG.",
f:{Role:"Bottle design, regular and refillable",Team:"International student team",Client:"Zlatna olive oil",Result:"Client chose our strategy and redesigns"},
b:"Our team researched the olive oil market across the EU and in specific countries. While the others built the rebranded business plan, I argued that the old bottle did not work in marketing terms and took on the redesign.",
d:["Suggested redesigning the bottle itself","Designed a new regular bottle","Designed a refillable bottle for customers who like refillables, made to look good in a kitchen"],
ex:[["Positioning and research", "Zlatna presents itself as a fairly luxurious olive oil brand, and different kinds of olive oil are aimed at different markets and buyers. The more luxurious the oil, the more the bottle has to look the part, so we made the new bottle look rich and expensive."], ["What we changed", "The original bottle was yellow, which we felt didn’t suit olive oil. A buyer should recognise at once that this is olive oil. The new regular bottle has a strict, rectangular shape with an olive branch, and it looks upmarket."], ["How the refillable bottle works", "The refillable bottle takes the shape of an olive. A customer buys it once, online or in a shop, and can then refill it with any oil. The idea is that it would be Zlatna’s oil, but for now that choice is left to the customer."]],
nohero:1,fl:{h:"From old to new",a:["zo","Before: the original Zlatna bottle."],b:["zn","After: the redesigned regular bottle."],c:["zr","In addition: a refillable bottle, shaped like an olive."]},g:[],
